NAME
       mkswap - prepare a device or file as swap space

SYNOPSIS
       mkswap [-L LABEL] [-U UUID] [-f] DEVICE|FILE

DESCRIPTION
       mkswap writes a swap signature on a partition, a logical volume or a
       file, so the kernel can use it as swap: space on disk where memory
       pages go when RAM runs short. It prints the size and the new UUID.

       mkswap only prepares the space. swapon turns it on now; a line in
       /etc/fstab turns it on at every boot:

           UUID=...   none   swap   defaults   0 0

       On an MBR or GPT partition, set the partition type to Linux swap too
       (fdisk type 82 / 19, gdisk 8200, parted "linux-swap"). This is good
       practice, though the kernel does not need it.

       A swap file must belong to root with mode 0600, and must be made with
       dd (not a sparse file) before mkswap runs on it.

OPTIONS
       -L, --label LABEL
              Give the swap space a label (use LABEL=... in fstab).

       -U, --uuid UUID
              Use this UUID instead of a random one.

       -f, --force
              Go ahead even on a whole disk with a partition table, or when
              the size looks wrong.

       -c, --check
              Check the device for bad blocks first.

EXAMPLES
       Add a 512 MiB swap partition non-destructively and enable it at
       every boot (on a GPT disk, where the first word is the name):

           parted /dev/vdb mkpart swap1 linux-swap 1001MiB 1513MiB
           mkswap /dev/vdb2
           echo 'UUID=<the UUID shown> none swap defaults 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
           swapon -a
           swapon --show

       Swap on a logical volume:

           lvcreate -n swap2 -L 256M vgdata
           mkswap /dev/vgdata/swap2
           swapon /dev/vgdata/swap2

       A swap file:

           dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=256
           chmod 600 /swapfile
           mkswap /swapfile
           swapon /swapfile

SEE ALSO
       swapon(8), swapoff(8), free(1), fstab(5), blkid(8)
